eadows","Ojos Negros is a popular checkpoint of the Baja 1000"],"supply":"No water between the south end of Tecate and Ejido Sierra Juárez (~100 km); next ATM only in Vicente Guerrero"},{"n":3,"from":124,"to":201,"title":"Ojos Negros → Santo Tomás","highlights":["Well-used Baja 1000 tracks","First view of the Pacific since the border from the high point after Tres Hermanos","Huge, initially rough descent to Ejido Uruapan (hot springs)","Santo Tomás valley is known for its wine"],"supply":"Small stores in Ejido Uruapan and Santo Tomás"},{"n":4,"from":201,"to":289,"title":"Santo Tomás → Colonet","highlights":["Steep switchback climb out of Santo Tomás","Scenic dirt roads along the rocky, wild Pacific coast – a stark contrast to developed Southern California","Fishing town Eréndira","Colonet: first of many colourful MEX1 highway towns – loncherías, panaderías, taco stands"],"supply":"Colonet is the last major resupply before Vicente Guerrero"},{"n":5,"from":289,"to":421,"title":"Colonet → Vicente Guerrero","highlights":["Sandy arroyo and stream crossings up from Ejido Benito Juárez","Switchbacks to the first big views of the Sierra San Pedro Mártir","Rancho El Coyote and Rancho Meling – backcountry ranches of the Meling family; the paved road at Meling leads to the observatory and Baja's highest peak","Technical doubletrack after Rancho Meling","Vicente Guerrero: FASS Bike, the best bike shop between Ensenada and La Paz"],"supply":"Carry food from Colonet; Vicente Guerrero has banks, motels, stores"},{"n":6,"from":421,"to":492,"title":"Vicente Guerrero → Nueva Odisea","highlights":["San Quintín coastal plain – highly productive farmland thanks to sea moisture and sunshine","Mellow dirt roads and shoreline trails, around low volcanoes","Salt flats, salt marshes and several miles of broad beach before Nueva Odisea","Two singletrack MTB centres near Vicente Guerrero and San Quintín"],"supply":"San Quintín: last ATM until Vizcaíno (~730 km later)"},{"n":7,"from":492,"to":688,"title":"Nueva Odisea → Cataviña","highlights":["Remote backcountry valleys with towering cirio trees and cardón cacti – the best of the Valle de los Cirios Biosphere","Rough jeep tracks and some soft sand","Family restaurant at El Sacrificio on MEX1","Cataviña is known for its huge granite boulders"],"supply":"No reliable water from Nueva Odisea until MEX1"},{"n":8,"from":688,"to":881,"title":"Cataviña → Santa Rosalillita","highlights":["Longest section of the whole Baja Divide without resupply and without water (~200 km)","Steep ridges through a protected area with some of the highest plant density on the route, species living off coastal moisture","Reach the Pacific at the fish camp San José del Faro – lobster or crab in season","Wild coast with the Seven Sisters surf breaks","Santa Rosalillita: active fishing village"],"supply":"8–12 litres of water and 2–3 days of food from Cataviña"},{"n":9,"from":881,"to":1005,"title":"Santa Rosalillita → Bahía de los Ángeles","highlights":["Some of the best coastal riding of the route south of Santa Rosalillita","Pacific and Sea of Cortez in one section","Misión San Borja – well-preserved mission church still in use, hot springs and cave paintings nearby","At last: the Sea of Cortez at Bahía de los Ángeles (BOLA)"],"supply":"Stores in Rosarito and BOLA; no bank in BOLA"},{"n":10,"from":1005,"to":1226,"title":"Bahía de los Ángeles → Vizcaíno","highlights":["Wide open desert on mostly good roads","Back to the sea at the fishing spot San Rafael","Rancho Escondido at El Progreso – family ecotourism ranch","El Arco: small, declining factory town","Dead-straight sandy track after El Arco (7–10 miles) – 3\" tyres recommended","Vizcaíno: cheap motels, great street food and ceviche, between Pacific and Sea of Cortez"],"supply":"Very limited between BOLA and Vizcaíno; ATM in Vizcaíno"},{"n":11,"from":1226,"to":1332,"title":"Vizcaíno → San Ignacio","highlights":["Expansive desert with big agricultural operations (El Caracol, Rancho Guadalupe)","Deep sand in places","Drainages from the Sierra de San Francisco","Final miles into San Ignacio wind through freshwater lagoons and date palms","San Ignacio: one of the two great oases and mission sites of Baja, stunning mission church on a shaded plaza"],"supply":"Stores and campgrounds in San Ignacio"},{"n":12,"from":1332,"to":1480,"title":"San Ignacio → El Dátil","highlights":["Quiet paved road to Laguna San Ignacio – grey whale watching January to April","Flat, fast hard-packed tracks across dry lake beds – with tailwind almost effortless","El Dátil: a real fishing community, seafood informally for sale"],"supply":"Small stores in San Zacarías, La Laguna, El Dátil"},{"n":13,"from":1480,"to":1594,"title":"El Dátil → Mulegé","highlights":["Coast-to-coast crossing of the peninsula","Canyon scenery with persistent surface water from La Ballena to San Miguel","Very steep descent down the Sierra de la Giganta toward the Sea of Cortez","Mulegé: shaded oasis, mission church, estuary and lighthouse"],"supply":"Resupply in El Dátil; full services in Mulegé"},{"n":14,"from":1594,"to":1691,"title":"Mulegé → El Rosarito","highlights":["Boat ride with local fishermen (panga) across Bahía Concepción to Los Hornitos","Faint track along the wild east side of the bay that few bikes have ridden, seasonal fish camps","Supreme views and wild camping","El Rosarito: busy roadside eatery popular with truckers"],"supply":"Almost nothing until San Nicolás; Mulegé is the last place for specialty goods before Constitución"},{"n":15,"from":1691,"to":1958,"title":"El Rosarito → Ciudad Constitución","highlights":["Mountain riding through the Sierra de la Giganta, linking Spanish mission sites","Palm groves and freshwater streams in deep canyons","Awesome descent into San José de Comondú","Misión San Javier – one of the most visited mission sites outside the cities","Citrus groves on the way into Ciudad Constitución, the commercial hub of the region"],"supply":"Only very small stores along the way"},{"n":16,"from":1958,"to":2235,"title":"Ciudad Constitución → La Paz","highlights":["Historic mission at San Luis Gonzaga","Long climb out of the low country into deep drainages and spectacular canyons","Pass in a nature preserve with views of the Sea of Cortez and a 600 m descent","Fishing village San Evaristo","Brilliant geology between San Evaristo and San Juan de la Costa – a colourful layer cake of rock","Arrival in La Paz along the Malecón"],"supply":"Extremely limited resupply until San Juan de la Costa"},{"n":17,"from":2235,"to":2378,"title":"La Paz → La Ribera","highlights":["Climb to ~600 m out of La Paz, view from the pass above Los Divisaderos over San Juan de los Planes and the Sea of Cortez","Singletrack loop into La Ventana – a world-famous kite and wind-sports spot","Some of the most scenic rocky coastal roads on the Sea of Cortez","East Cape towns Los Barriles and La Ribera"],"supply":"Good resupply in La Ventana, Los Barriles, La Ribera"},{"n":18,"from":2378,"to":2489,"title":"La Ribera → San José del Cabo","highlights":["Cabo Pulmo – singletrack and snorkelling at the national park","Sandy climb away from the sea after Los Frailes","Big views of Mt. Trinidad and the Sierra de la Laguna","Long descent to the finish in San José del Cabo"],"supply":"Hardly anything between Cabo Pulmo and Palo Escopeta"}],"track":[[0,32.5753,-116.6274],[2,32.5623,-116.6314],[4,32.5488,-116.6292],[6,32.5358,-116.636],[8,32.5219,-116.6257],[10,32.5131,-116.6083],[12,32.5044,-116.5951],[14,32.4954,-116.5778],[16,32.4818,-116.566],[18,32.4906,-116.5492],[20,32.4849,-116.5345],[22,32.4764,-116.5254],[24,32.4641,-116.5111],[26,32.4559,-116.4954],[28,32.4451,-116.486],[30,32.4391,-116.4692],[32,32.4286,-116.4532],[34,32.419,-116.4366],[36,32.421,-116.4185],[38,32.4267,-116.4],[40,32.4286,-116.3831],[42,32.4215,-116.3649],[44,32.4196,-116.3444],[46,32.4095,-116.3293],[48,32.3995,-116.3185],[50,32.3863,-116.3041],[52,32.3718,-116.2921],[54,32.3679,-116.2733]